findings.problem:
__init_subclass__ walks reversed(cls.__mro__) and MUTATES the inherited Lever OBJECT, setting .prefix on the instance the base class still holds. A second subclass silently retargets the first one's environment name. Reproduced:

  class Base(LeverSet):  PREFIX="BASE"; x = Lever(1, ...)
  Base.env_names()  -> {'BASE_X'}
  class Child(Base): PREFIX="CHILD"; y = Lever(2, ...)
  Base.env_names()  -> {'CHILD_X'}          # was BASE_X
  Base.from_env({'BASE_X':'9','CHILD_X':'7'}).x  ->  7   # expected 9

Every downstream guarantee breaks at once: BASE_X is still in registry._ENV_OWNER as owned by BASE, so unread_env() does NOT report it as a typo while setting it does nothing; affects('BASE_X') returns a reach for a name nothing reads (the L3 sweep goes green on it forever); and the base package silently reads the child's knob. This is not a hypothetical shape -- tests/test_ownership.py:_leversets() resolves lever sets to a fixed point precisely because 'a package may reasonably declare an intermediate base (class Cadenced(LeverSet))'. The design anticipates inheritance; the mechanism is broken for it.
